I just did a lot of looking around before I got my new laptop a few 
months ago.  I wanted a decent sound and video system, as well as 
Linux/*BSD support; decent price is a plus.

I went with an MSI-1029.  It's listed on several Linux-laptop web sites 
as well as a FreeBSD site as working well with Open Source systems 
(except the WinModem, but I can live with having to boot to Windows for 
that).  The base price is around $900, but fully loaded (2GHz Turion64 
CPU, 2G RAM, 120G disk) and a spare power adapter it cost a tad less 
that $2k.

You can get them with Linux pre-installed from some of the online 
resellers, but those seem to all have pretty bad customer service 
records (as well as seeming to be all the same company behind the 
scenes; it's weird).  I got mine from <http://www.rawpowerpc.com>; they 
had really good ratings, and I had a very good experience with them.

I haven't had time to do more than install Windows and OpenBSD; since I 
need to actually use it right now I haven't had time to set up the 
X-Windows.  I can report that the ethernet and wireless cards both work 
fine in OpenBSD, though, and others have said that the video and sound 
do as well.

Of course, it's ludicrously over-powered just for running NetHack, but 
what the heck.  ;-)

